{"id":"astr-101-final","course":"ASTR 101","kind":"final","title":"Final Exam","questions":[{"id":"f-q1","prompt":"Keating presents astronomy chiefly as:","choices":["A A 21st-century hobby only.","B Humanity's oldest science.","C Pure fortune-telling.","D Cosmetology."],"answer":1,"cite":"1.2","explain":"Oldest-science framing opens the course."},{"id":"f-q2","prompt":"Greek astron + nomos, for Keating, points to:","choices":["A Rocket fuel chemistry.","B Star-naming / star culture.","C Ocean tides only.","D Plate tectonics."],"answer":1,"cite":"1.5","explain":"Etymology of astronomy as star culture."},{"id":"f-q3","prompt":"An early quantitative astronomical thinker he highlights is:","choices":["A Aristotle.","B Only Elon Musk.","C Doppler alone.","D Vera Rubin alone."],"answer":0,"cite":"1.31","explain":"Aristotle is named early for quantitative thought/logic."},{"id":"f-q4","prompt":"Astronomical messengers include electromagnetic radiation—i.e.:","choices":["A Only smell.","B Light of all wavelengths (among other channels).","C Only seismic waves.","D Only neutrinos forever."],"answer":1,"cite":"1.51","explain":"EM radiation is the first of the forms he lists."},{"id":"f-q5","prompt":"Eratosthenes' Earth circumference vs modern:","choices":["A ~46,000 km then vs ~40,000 km now.","B Exact match always.","C He measured Mars.","D Flat-Earth proof."],"answer":0,"cite":"1.101","explain":"Keating compares ~46k vs ~40k km."},{"id":"f-q6","prompt":"Speed of light / EM waves in Keating's lecture is about:","choices":["A 300,000 km/s.","B 300 m/s.","C 3 km/h.","D Infinite in glass."],"answer":0,"cite":"2.5","explain":"Canonical c ≈ 300,000 km/s."},{"id":"f-q7","prompt":"Simplest refractors resemble:","choices":["A Only radio dishes.","B The human eye with focusing lenses.","C Particle colliders.","D Submarines."],"answer":1,"cite":"2.33","explain":"Eye analogy for refractors."},{"id":"f-q8","prompt":"Reflecting telescopes are credited to:","choices":["A Newton.","B Aristotle.","C Ptolemy.","D Brahe only."],"answer":0,"cite":"2.45","explain":"Newton's reflector."},{"id":"f-q9","prompt":"Biggest historical astronomical lenses ~:","choices":["A 1 m; space scopes can be larger.","B 100 km.","C Nanometers only.","D No lenses exist."],"answer":0,"cite":"2.53","explain":"~1 m lens limit noted."},{"id":"f-q10","prompt":"Doppler's contribution emphasized here:","choices":["A Wavelength shift from motion.","B Inventing glass.","C Naming planets.","D Proving flat Earth."],"answer":0,"cite":"2.89","explain":"Motion → wavelength shift."},{"id":"f-q11","prompt":"Voyager 2 distance scale check:","choices":["A ~1 light-day out; nearest star ~4+ ly.","B Already at Andromeda.","C Still on the Moon.","D Beyond the observable universe."],"answer":0,"cite":"3.3","explain":"Light-day vs light-years contrast."},{"id":"f-q12","prompt":"A parsec is fundamentally:","choices":["A A parallax-based distance unit (AU baseline).","B A unit of luminosity.","C A comet type.","D A calendar month."],"answer":0,"cite":"3.36","explain":"Parsec via parallax geometry."},{"id":"f-q13","prompt":"Earth–ecliptic tilt ~:","choices":["A 23°, linked to seasons.","B 0°.","C 90°.","D 360°."],"answer":0,"cite":"3.61","explain":"~23° tilt."},{"id":"f-q14","prompt":"Minimum stellar mass fraction of the Sun:","choices":["A ~8%.","B ~0.01%.","C Exactly Jupiter.","D 200%."],"answer":0,"cite":"3.81","explain":"~8% solar mass threshold."},{"id":"f-q15","prompt":"Planet crossing star disk method:","choices":["A Transit method.","B Only tasting starlight.","C Counting sunspots by hand.","D Measuring Earth's age only."],"answer":0,"cite":"3.96","explain":"Transits as mini-eclipses."},{"id":"f-q16","prompt":"Stellar energy processes emphasized:","choices":["A Nuclear processes capable of self-sustaining yield.","B Only candle wax.","C Pure magnetism.","D Geothermal vents on Earth only."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.3","explain":"Nuclear vs chemical framing."},{"id":"f-q17","prompt":"What compresses solar-core material for fusion?","choices":["A Gravity.","B Tiny pumps.","C Human reactors.","D Ocean tides."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.17","explain":"Gravity-driven fusion."},{"id":"f-q18","prompt":"Solar rotation via sunspots ~:","choices":["A 28 Earth days.","B 1 hour.","C 1000 years.","D Instantaneous."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.53","explain":"~28-day solar rotation."},{"id":"f-q19","prompt":"Iron fusion in massive stars:","choices":["A Costs more energy than it returns → collapse.","B Powers stars forever.","C Makes only water.","D Happens only in labs."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.57","explain":"Endothermic iron fusion ends hydrostatic support."},{"id":"f-q20","prompt":"Retrograde planetary motion challenged:","choices":["A Simple Earth-centered models (Ptolemy/Aristotle era).","B Only weather forecasts.","C Music theory.","D Plate tectonics."],"answer":0,"cite":"4.65","explain":"Retrograde forced epicycle complexity."},{"id":"f-q21","prompt":"Galileo vs Aristotle on falling:","choices":["A All masses fall at the same rate.","B Heavy always faster.","C Nothing falls.","D Only feathers fall."],"answer":0,"cite":"5.15","explain":"Equal free-fall rates."},{"id":"f-q22","prompt":"Newtonian gravity's distance law:","choices":["A Inverse square.","B Inverse tenth.","C Constant force forever.","D Random."],"answer":0,"cite":"5.21","explain":"1/r² behavior."},{"id":"f-q23","prompt":"Etymology of \"galaxy\":","choices":["A Greek milky-circle roots.","B Klingon.","C Acronym for GPS.","D Latin for comet."],"answer":0,"cite":"5.41","explain":"Milky circle etymology."},{"id":"f-q24","prompt":"Herschel's early map put us:","choices":["A At the galactic center.","B Outside the Local Group.","C Inside the Sun.","D On Pluto."],"answer":0,"cite":"5.43","explain":"Center placement / cosmic ego."},{"id":"f-q25","prompt":"1920 debate pair:","choices":["A Shapley & Curtis.","B Bohr & Heisenberg only.","C Darwin & Wallace.","D Bach & Mozart."],"answer":0,"cite":"5.49","explain":"Great Debate names."},{"id":"f-q26","prompt":"Cepheids help because of:","choices":["A Period–luminosity relation.","B Random noise only.","C Being invisible always.","D Fixed absolute magnitude 1000."],"answer":0,"cite":"6.10","explain":"Period tracks luminosity."},{"id":"f-q27","prompt":"Key Cepheid pioneer named:","choices":["A Henrietta Swan Leavitt.","B Only Aristotle.","C Tycho alone.","D Cardano."],"answer":0,"cite":"6.22","explain":"Leavitt's Magellanic work."},{"id":"f-q28","prompt":"Rubin/Ford curves imply:","choices":["A Dark matter.","B No gravity.","C Empty galaxies.","D Flat Earth."],"answer":0,"cite":"6.43","explain":"Rotation curves → dark matter."},{"id":"f-q29","prompt":"WIMP expands to:","choices":["A Weakly interacting massive particle.","B Wildly infinite mass photon.","C Weekly infrared mapping.","D Weighted integer math problem."],"answer":0,"cite":"6.64","explain":"WIMP acronym."},{"id":"f-q30","prompt":"\"Star stuff\" punchline:","choices":["A Mostly water / Big Bang products.","B Pure gold.","C Only silicon.","D Vacuum energy alone."],"answer":0,"cite":"6.78","explain":"Water / Big Bang stuff joke."},{"id":"f-q31","prompt":"Olbers' paradox concerns:","choices":["A Why night is dark in a naive infinite starry cosmos.","B Why days are short in winter only.","C Comet colors.","D Lens grinding."],"answer":0,"cite":"7.3","explain":"Dark night sky paradox."},{"id":"f-q32","prompt":"Hubble's key synthesis:","choices":["A Distance–recession relation from Cepheids + redshifts.","B Proving epicycles.","C Inventing glass.","D Naming WIMPs."],"answer":0,"cite":"7.37","explain":"Hubble–Leavitt expansion evidence."},{"id":"f-q33","prompt":"Hubble constant units:","choices":["A km/s/Mpc.","B kg/m³.","C candela.","D pascal."],"answer":0,"cite":"7.49","explain":"km s⁻¹ Mpc⁻¹."},{"id":"f-q34","prompt":"Backward expansion implies:","choices":["A A denser early state / Big Bang pillar.","B Eternal static crystal spheres.","C Instant Earth-only creation myths as physics.","D No cosmology."],"answer":0,"cite":"7.53","explain":"Run movie backward."},{"id":"f-q35","prompt":"Early rapid expansion idea named:","choices":["A Cosmic inflation.","B Steady alchemy.","C Phlogiston.","D Geocentrism."],"answer":0,"cite":"7.65","explain":"Inflation conjecture."},{"id":"f-q36","prompt":"Tycho's comet work used:","choices":["A Parallax constraints on comet distances/nature.","B Only horoscopes.","C Neutrino detectors.","D Stock tips."],"answer":0,"cite":"8.3","explain":"Parallax vs atmospheric-comet ideas."},{"id":"f-q37","prompt":"Widmanstätten patterns appear in:","choices":["A Etched iron meteorite slices.","B Fresh bread.","C Soap bubbles only.","D Neon signs."],"answer":0,"cite":"8.19","explain":"Meteorite crystallography."},{"id":"f-q38","prompt":"Earth water delivery timing:","choices":["A Post-Theia cometary bombardment.","B Before any planets.","C Only last century.","D From human plumbing."],"answer":0,"cite":"8.55","explain":"Comets after Moon-forming impact."},{"id":"f-q39","prompt":"1950s abiogenesis experiments aimed to make:","choices":["A Amino acids from nonliving chemistry.","B Black holes.","C Telescopes.","D GPS satellites."],"answer":0,"cite":"8.73","explain":"Miller–Urey-style chemistry."},{"id":"f-q40","prompt":"Breakthrough Listen uses:","choices":["A Large radio telescopes hunting technosignatures.","B Only optical microscopes.","C Seismometers exclusively.","D Alchemy labs."],"answer":0,"cite":"8.91","explain":"Radio SETI breakthrough project."}]}